<p>One of the most useful questions before ordering is <strong>how much Wagyu to buy per person</strong>. Conventional steak portions can be misleading here. A 300- or 400-gram serving may work for other beef, but highly marbled Japanese Wagyu delivers far more fat richness and intensity in each bite.</p>
<p>There is no universal official serving size. Cut, BMS, menu structure and appetite all matter. As a practical culinary guide, <strong>roughly 60 to 120 grams per person covers many Japanese A5 tasting and menu situations</strong>, while larger portions may suit less extreme marbling or a meal where the beef is the only main course.</p>
<h2>Why Wagyu needs a different portion logic</h2>
<p>In highly marbled Wagyu, intramuscular fat melts quickly and gives every bite a rich, lingering mouthfeel. Satiety can therefore arrive sooner than with lean beef. The right amount should not simply copy a conventional steak serving.</p>
<h2>For a tasting: about 40-70 grams per person</h2>
<p>If several people are sampling Japanese Wagyu as part of a tasting, <strong>40 to 70 grams each</strong> can provide several meaningful bites. This works well for a first experience or for comparing two cuts.</p>
<h2>Within a multi-course menu: about 70-100 grams</h2>
<p>When Wagyu is an important course but there are starters, sides and dessert, <strong>70 to 100 grams per person</strong> is a useful reference for highly marbled beef. It is substantial without turning the meal into an exercise in richness.</p>
<h2>As the main course: roughly 100-150 grams</h2>
<p>If Wagyu is the centre of the meal, consider around <strong>100 to 150 grams per person</strong>. For very high-BMS A5, 100 grams may be generous. A less extreme grade or cut may make a somewhat larger serving comfortable.</p>
<h2>BMS changes how much people tend to want</h2>
<p>A5 can include BMS 8 through 12. As marbling becomes more intense, smaller portions often make more sense. An A4 or less extreme A5 may feel more suitable in a larger serving because lean meat remains more prominent.</p>
<h2>The cut matters too</h2>
<p>Highly marbled ribeye may feel richer than a cut with a different muscle-to-fat balance. Tenderloin, picanha, striploin and other cuts each behave differently, so gram ranges are only a starting point.</p>
<h2>For two people</h2>
<p>For a first A5 tasting with other food, <strong>120 to 160 grams total</strong> can work. If Wagyu is the main event, roughly 200 to 250 grams shared can be generous without forcing an oversized serving.</p>
<h2>For four people</h2>
<p>For a tasting, around 200 to 280 grams total provides 50-70 grams each. For a more substantial Wagyu course, 80-100 grams per person means roughly 320-400 grams.</p>
<h2>For six or eight people</h2>
<p>At 50-60 grams per person, six diners need about 300-360 grams and eight about 400-480 grams. Increase the amount if Wagyu is a larger course rather than a tasting.</p>
<h2>One large piece or several small ones?</h2>
<p>A large piece can be easy to portion evenly, while several smaller pieces let you compare cuts and control cooking more precisely. For a first purchase, <strong>getting the quantity right matters more than maximising size</strong>.</p>
<h2>If you are serving several meats</h2>
<p>Reduce the Wagyu portion. In a comparative beef tasting, 40-60 grams of Wagyu per person can be enough to demonstrate the contrast. Highly marbled A5 works well as a climax rather than necessarily the largest serving.</p>
<h2>Sides can balance a smaller portion</h2>
<p>Simple rice, vegetables, mild pickles or fresh acidic elements can refresh the palate. Avoid loading the plate with additional heavy fats when the Wagyu is already extremely marbled.</p>
<h2>A simple calculation method</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>40-70 g:</strong> tasting or comparison.</li>
<li><strong>70-100 g:</strong> featured course within a menu.</li>
<li><strong>100-150 g:</strong> Wagyu main course, adjusted for marbling and appetite.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are culinary guidelines, not official rules. Very high-BMS A5 may call for less; lower marbling or trimming loss may call for more.</p>
<h2>The best serving leaves you wanting another bite</h2>
<p>With such a rich and expensive ingredient, slightly under-serving is often better than overwhelming the palate. Think in terms of <strong>intensity rather than volume</strong>.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is 100 grams of A5 Wagyu enough per person?</h3>
<p>For many diners, yes, particularly when the beef is highly marbled and served within a broader menu.</p>
<h3>Can I serve 200 grams per person?</h3>
<p>You can, but very highly marbled A5 may feel excessively rich in that amount.</p>
<h3>How much for a four-person tasting?</h3>
<p>About 200-280 grams total is a practical starting point.</p>
<h3>Do I need more if it is A4?</h3>
<p>Not automatically, but a less extreme fat profile may make a slightly larger portion enjoyable.</p>
